A note to my future self—the one who might be panicking during a crash or getting a big head during a boom.
We often hear the saying, &ldquo;The stock market is a form of spiritual discipline.&rdquo; But for most of us, it’s less about Zen and more about a severe case of &ldquo;investment schizophrenia.&rdquo;
If you look back at the 2000 Dot-Com Bubble or the 2008 Financial Crisis, countless investors kept repeating the same tired script: They got greedy when they should have been terrified, and terrified when they should have been greedy.
I’m writing this down as a permanent reminder to my future self: When the market starts swinging wildly, don&rsquo;t fall into the psychological trap that 70% of people do—the endless flip-flop between the &ldquo;Cocky Gambler&rdquo; and the &ldquo;Defeated Student.&rdquo;
1. 🚨 Watch Out for the &ldquo;Bull Market Gambler&rdquo;: When I think &ldquo;Buffett is Overrated&rdquo; When your account balance is constantly in the green and your net worth hits a new high every day, you need to seriously watch out for that &ldquo;Gambler Persona&rdquo; whispering in your ear.
The world suddenly looks perfect: everything you buy shoots up, and all the news is fantastic. That’s when a dangerous illusion creeps in: &ldquo;Maybe I&rsquo;m actually a genius? Buffett only makes 20% a year, but I pulled 50% this month! Is his old-school stuff even relevant anymore?&rdquo;
Remember this:
That&rsquo;s the wind, not your wings. Most of your profit in a bull market is $\beta$ (Beta) return, which is just the market rising, or &ldquo;a rising tide lifting all boats.&rdquo; You made that 50% because you were standing in the wind tunnel, not because you suddenly grew the ability to fly. Never confuse luck with destiny. The worst thing that can happen to a gambler is winning the first few bets. If you pat yourself on the back for winning a huge, risky bet, you’re just reinforcing a wrong idea in your brain. This &ldquo;mysterious confidence&rdquo; will make you bet even bigger next time, until you give back everything you earned, plus your original capital. A Warning to Myself: The moment you start looking down on conservative, long-term investors, and when you think risk management is for cowards, your mindset has already crashed, even if your account hasn&rsquo;t. As soon as the tide rolls out, you’ll be the one swimming naked.
2. 🆘 Saving the &ldquo;Bear Market Student&rdquo;: When I want to Sell Everything and Swear Off Stocks Forever When the cycle flips, a black swan lands, and your account drops by 30%, 40%, or even 50%, a different personality takes over—the &ldquo;Defeated Student Persona.&rdquo;
Like a student who fails test after test, you&rsquo;ll fall into a deep state of Learned Helplessness. You’ll start beating yourself up: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m so stupid, I got greedy,&rdquo; &ldquo;The stock market is a scam,&rdquo; &ldquo;I&rsquo;m done with stocks, I&rsquo;m taking the rest of my money out and putting it in a savings account.&rdquo;
This is the exact feeling at the market bottom in 2008. But you must remember: this is the moment that demands the most courage.
A loss is a cost, not a sign of incompetence. How can you run a business without buying inventory? A temporary paper loss on a stock is just the &ldquo;inventory cost&rdquo; you pay for potential future returns. As long as the logic of the asset you bought hasn&rsquo;t changed, its value hasn&rsquo;t changed either. Don&rsquo;t chop down a tree in the winter. Students fail because they lack ability, but the stock market crashes mostly because of emotional collapse. When others are in peak panic and throwing in the towel (selling at a loss), that’s usually when assets are on the deepest discount. A Warning to Myself: If your immediate feeling after a huge drop is &ldquo;shame&rdquo; and &ldquo;I need to run away,&rdquo; instead of &ldquo;excitement&rdquo; and &ldquo;I need to look for gold in the wreckage,&rdquo; you have completely wasted a crisis. The real transfer of wealth always happens at this exact moment.
3. 🤖 Becoming the Third Person: The Cold, Hard Probability Manager To survive in this brutal market, I need to kill both the &ldquo;Gambler&rdquo; and the &ldquo;Student&rdquo; inside me. I must become an Operator.
How to Handle a Bull Market: Be extremely humble. Acknowledge the role of luck, and constantly ask: Is my profit based on taking on too much risk? If the market crashed tomorrow, would my investment logic still hold up? How to Handle a Bear Market: Be extremely rational and cold. Strip away all fear and focus only on valuation and probability. If a great company is trading at a 50% discount because of panic, I need to be the buyer, no matter who is selling. Final Takeaway
Future me, whether you are currently feeling high as a kite looking at your double-bagger account, or sobbing while looking at your position that’s been cut in half, please come back here and read this one last sentence:
Don’t look for validation in good times, and don’t look for defeat in bad times. Be a cold-blooded killer who is accountable only to &ldquo;value,&rdquo; and never pays the price for &ldquo;emotion.&rdquo;

To keep up with the times and experience the freshness of IPv6, I plan to enable IPv6 on my secondary internal network.
Because my home network has two tiers: the first tier is the main router, which can obtain IPv6 and IPv6-PD assigned by the carrier and can assign a public IPv6 to devices connected to it. The second tier is the one in my study, which can only obtain a public IPv6 itself, and the devices connected to it only get an internal IPv6.
Open the secondary router&rsquo;s OpenWRT settings -&gt; Interface -&gt; WAN6 -&gt; DHCP Server -&gt; IPv6 Settings.
Set the Router Advertisement (RA) service, DHCPv6 service, and NDP Proxy all to relay mode, and check the option &ldquo;Designated master.&rdquo;
Designated master (checked) Set this interface as master for RA and DHCPv6 relaying as well as NDP proxying. RA-Service relay mode Configures the operation mode of the RA service on this interface. DHCPv6-Service relay mode Configures the operation mode of the DHCPv6 service on this interface. NDP-Proxy relay mode Then open OpenWRT settings -&gt; Interface -&gt; LAN -&gt; DHCP Server -&gt; IPv6 Settings.
Set the Router Advertisement (RA) service, DHCPv6 service, and NDP Proxy all to relay mode. Note that this time, do not check &ldquo;Designated master.&rdquo;
Finally, save and apply everything, and then test IPv6 again. I found that IPv6 can now be obtained normally.
Final UCI configuration:
root@OpenWRT-MY:~# uci show dhcp.lan dhcp.lan=dhcp dhcp.lan.interface=&#39;lan&#39; dhcp.lan.start=&#39;100&#39; dhcp.lan.limit=&#39;150&#39; dhcp.lan.leasetime=&#39;12h&#39; dhcp.lan.dhcpv4=&#39;server&#39; dhcp.lan.ra=&#39;relay&#39; dhcp.lan.dhcpv6=&#39;relay&#39; dhcp.lan.ndp=&#39;relay&#39; root@OpenWRT-MY:~# uci show dhcp.wan6 dhcp.wan6=dhcp dhcp.wan6.interface=&#39;wan6&#39; dhcp.wan6.ignore=&#39;1&#39; dhcp.wan6.master=&#39;1&#39; dhcp.wan6.ra=&#39;relay&#39; dhcp.wan6.dhcpv6=&#39;relay&#39; dhcp.wan6.ndp=&#39;relay&#39;

 “Don&rsquo;t be the loudest sheep, and don&rsquo;t be the fattest sheep.”
I. Recognizing the Essence: Don&rsquo;t be the &ldquo;Awakened and Angry Sheep&rdquo; Anger is for the shepherd to see; ultimately, there are only two outcomes:
Being slaughtered → Establishing dominance (or: Asserting authority) Being penned → Being drained (or: Being exploited) Many &ldquo;awakened sheep&rdquo; simply become &ldquo;negative examples for display.&rdquo;
→ Strategy: Remain low-key, be clear-headed internally, be ambiguous externally, and avoid becoming the &ldquo;leader sheep.&rdquo;
II. Reducing the Degree of Harvesting: Hiding the Wool You provide a little bit of wool, pretending to be &ldquo;dutiful,&rdquo; but the real wool must be hidden where &ldquo;others cannot see it.&rdquo;
Specific real-world applications:
Diversified income sources (not relying on a single pasture) Asset relocation or secretive investments Skill reserves (not being in a role that can be replaced at any time) Mental independence (not participating in the mutual harm competition within the flock) → Strategy: &ldquo;I may not be the richest sheep, but I live freer than you.&rdquo;
III. Establishing the &ldquo;Sheep&rsquo;s Secret Passage&rdquo;: Being the Sheep with a Backdoor Historically, the sheep that lived comfortably were always &ldquo;amphibious&rdquo; (or: dual-natured): → On the surface, they are sheep, but secretly they dig a tunnel.
Technical immigration Overseas accounts Digital identity On-chain assets → Strategy: Never expose your entire self within the pasture system.
IV. Avoiding Internal Competition and Mutual Harm: Don&rsquo;t help the shepherd bite other sheep The most basic sheep like to step on others to lift themselves up, competing to be the &ldquo;leader sheep.&rdquo; In reality, this only gives the shepherd another handle to grab, and eventually, they are slaughtered anyway. Truly smart sheep signal to each other, form invisible small groups, share secret resources, and don&rsquo;t report each other. → Strategy: Maintain tacit understanding among the sheep, and try to unite &ldquo;like-minded awakened sheep,&rdquo; but without exposure.
Summary: &ldquo;Be the sheep that looks ordinary, contributes moderately, is calm internally, and has a way out (a backup plan).&rdquo;
&ldquo;While others are engaging in internal competition and mutual harm to become the &rsquo;leader sheep,&rsquo; I am already digging a small path leading to another pasture in the back hills.&rdquo;

If you can remain calm, when the people around you all go mad and accuse you,
If you can believe in yourself, when everyone doubts you—let them doubt.
If you endure waiting, yet are not weary from the wait; or if you suffer deception, yet do not repay it with lies; or if you are met with hatred, yet do not retaliate with hatred—able to remain composed when displeased, and able to avoid flattery&hellip;
If you can converse with common folk with courtesy, or walk alongside kings—without becoming subservient;
If both enemies and close friends cannot harm you;
If you care for everyone, yet cannot function if anyone is missing;
If you are thinking of venting your anger, go run for sixty seconds, and the earth and all things upon it will belong to you, And more importantly, you will be a true man, my child!
If you can combine a collection of knowledge patterns and answer after another why, you will be able to think more deeply; by the same token, if you tell people things, always tell them the reason, and they will understand what you say more profoundly, they will value what you say more highly, and they will be more inclined to follow what you say. Even if they do not understand your reasoning, they will be more inclined to listen to your words.

Patience: The Art of Patient Waiting Look at those hedge funds—do you think they know how to wait? They fundamentally don&rsquo;t know how to wait! In my personal portfolio, I have held U.S. Treasuries or municipal bonds ranging from $10 million to $12 million for several consecutive years, and then I just stopped touching them, just quietly waiting, waiting&hellip;
&ldquo;As Jesse Livermore said: &lsquo;The secret to making big money is not in buying and selling&hellip; but in waiting.&rsquo;&rdquo;
Charlie&rsquo;s Principles and Investment Philosophy Charlie believes that forethought, patience, self-discipline, and impartiality are the most fundamental guiding principles. No matter how the outside world views things, and no matter how my own emotions fluctuate, he always adheres to these principles. Although many people believe that &ldquo;one must learn to adapt to random circumstances,&rdquo; Charlie remains unshaken. If one can firmly adhere to these principles, they can demonstrate one of Munger&rsquo;s most famous traits: infrequent trading.
Like Buffett, Munger believes that just a few decisions are enough to achieve a successful investment career. Therefore, when Charlie identifies a company he likes, he makes a very large bet, and usually holds the stock of that company for the long term (see &ldquo;The Investment Performance of Charlie Munger&rsquo;s Partnership&rdquo; in Chapter One). Charlie calls this method the &ldquo;Sit-and-Wait Investment Strategy&rdquo; (or &ldquo;Patient Waiting Method&rdquo;) and clearly points out its advantages: &ldquo;You pay less in fees to the trader, and you hear less nonsense; if this method works, the tax system will give you an extra 1% to 3% return every year.&rdquo; In his view, buying just three stocks is enough. Therefore, Charlie is willing to commit a large proportion of capital to individual &ldquo;watched&rdquo; opportunities. No Wall Street institution, financial advisor, or open-end fund manager makes such a declaration!
Lessons from Warren&rsquo;s Investment Course &ldquo;When Warren was in business school, he said: &lsquo;I can improve your final financial situation with just one attendance card; this card has 20 slots, so you only have 20 chances to punch in throughout your life—this represents the number of times you can invest in your life. Once you punch the card, you can&rsquo;t invest anymore. Under this rule, you will truly consider every investment action; you will have to put large sums of money into the projects you truly want to invest in. This way, your performance will be much better.&rsquo;&rdquo;
The Rhythm and Strategy of Investing Quickly eliminate what should not be done, then launch a skilled, interdisciplinary attack on what should be done, and then, when the right opportunity comes—only when the right opportunity comes—act decisively.

Money is what you exchange your life energy for. You sell time to acquire money. Ned sells time for $100 an hour, and you sell time for $20 an hour—that doesn&rsquo;t matter. Ned&rsquo;s money has nothing to do with you. The only true wealth you possess is your time, every second and minute of your life.
You use some of that precious time to exchange for money (cash), but money is meaningless; your time is where all meaning and value reside.
&ldquo;Understanding that money is life energy will make you the master of money. You will clearly know how much life energy you are willing to sell to acquire money. Look around at what you have accumulated, and you can ask yourself: &lsquo;How much time from my life did I invest to own this&hellip; chair&hellip; car&hellip; set of cookware&hellip; diploma on the wall?&rsquo; See how this affects your next purchase.&rdquo;

Merton Malkiel&rsquo;s saying: &ldquo;Never buy from a panting person.&rdquo;
Warren Buffett also said something similar: &ldquo;Be wary of investment activities that make people cheer; true investment genius is usually very boring.&rdquo;
When trillions of dollars are managed by Wall Street professionals and charge high management fees, it is usually the managers who reap huge profits, not the clients.
Don&rsquo;t assume you know more than the market; no one knows more than the market. Don&rsquo;t act based on your own opinions; you think that is your idea alone, but in reality, millions of people think that way.&quot;
The lower the annual fee paid by investors, the more money left in their pocket.

High expectations are accompanied by high anxiety. They expect to prove their excellence again through their academic results, but the extreme anxiety within constantly tells them, &ldquo;You aren&rsquo;t trying hard enough, you aren&rsquo;t good enough, you can&rsquo;t do it.&rdquo; The energy that should be invested in studying is exhausted by this internal struggle and tearing, and their grades slide down like a snowball until one day, they lose the courage to even step into school.
I don&rsquo;t really want to sit in an office. I probably won&rsquo;t be very wealthy, and I have my own way of handling things—maybe I won&rsquo;t work that hard—but I feel my inner world is quite rich. If possible, I want to be a freelancer. As for what I will do in the future, I&rsquo;m actually not too concerned about it, nor am I worried, because I feel I can handle most things well. I think the world is still very beautiful, and there are so many things to pursue. After getting to know many people, I realized that I am a very happy person—very, very, very happy&hellip;
It&rsquo;s rare to find a 16-year-old high school student who isn&rsquo;t pursuing being better, but rather becoming a more complete person. The questions she contemplates are things that many adults might never think about in their entire lives.

The Market is Only Equivalent to a Person of Average Intelligence As a participant in the trading market, you are unlikely to sell at the peak, and it is also difficult to buy at the trough&hellip;
Why? The reason is very clear and simple:
Humans are Impulsive and Emotional The peak and the trough are both caused by the &ldquo;impulse&rdquo; of a small portion of traders&hellip;
You are destined to be a non-impulsive person, so that impulsive trading is destined not to belong to the non-impulsive you! Believe me, you are a normal person, so it is very normal for you to occasionally experience a little hallucination. However, you are different. Where is the difference? The difference is that when the hallucination appears, you can identify it, clearly know that it is a hallucination, and therefore, just like a normal person, you can shake your head vigorously, shake off that hallucination, and continue living and thinking normally&hellip;

People Dislike Hearing Bad News Or anything that contradicts their existing views and conclusions. Therefore, if certain things might be painful, the human brain will start to act and try to deny reality. Psychologist Daniel Kahneman, a Nobel laureate, believes: &ldquo;People don&rsquo;t even want to put in the minimal effort to figure out what they did wrong; this is not accidental, but because they simply don&rsquo;t want to know.&rdquo;
Everyone Avoids It We all invent stories for ourselves and evade the facts. Even if you spend a lot of time studying behavioral economics, your skills might only improve slightly. You will always make mistakes. People often ignore or refuse to admit the faults of those they love, and tend to distort the facts to accommodate their loved ones.
Some people are clearly criminals, obvious without needing analysis or inference, yet their mother insists that her son is innocent. This is simple psychological denial. Reality is too painful to bear, so you must distort it in order to endure it. To some extent, we all do this; it is a common psychological misjudgment, but it causes serious problems.

You revere natural law, he worships authority, This is a difference in worldview;
You stand on the side of conscience, he stands on the side of the strong, This is a difference in values;
Your effort is for an ideal life, His effort is to be superior to others, This is a difference in philosophy of life.

 Fallacy of Assertion: In this fallacy, the assertion does not provide sufficient evidence or support, but merely claims a fact as a declaration. For example: &ldquo;Everyone knows I am the smartest person.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Causation: This fallacy lies in incorrectly assuming a causal relationship between two events. For example: &ldquo;I ate strawberry ice cream, so it caused the weather to get cold.&rdquo; This fallacy ignores other factors that might influence the weather.
Ad Hominem Fallacy: In this fallacy, the arguer is attacked, rather than the argument itself being rationally debated. For example: &ldquo;This plan is unwise because it was proposed by a foolish person.&rdquo;
Appeal to Emotion: In this fallacy, one attempts to persuade people to accept a certain argument by exciting their emotions rather than through logic and evidence. For example: &ldquo;Support this policy, and let&rsquo;s create a better future together.&rdquo;
Appeal to Invalid Authority: In this fallacy, one attempts to use an invalid or irrelevant authority to support an argument. For example: &ldquo;Celebrity X likes this product, so it must be good.&rdquo;
Hasty Generalization: This fallacy involves drawing overly broad conclusions based on limited evidence or samples. For example: &ldquo;I saw two people who didn&rsquo;t like this movie, so everyone won&rsquo;t like it.&rdquo;
False Analogy: In this fallacy, an argument is supported by making an inappropriate or unsuitable comparison. For example: &ldquo;Feeding vegetables to children is like feeding them to pets; it&rsquo;s all for their own good.&rdquo;
Chain of Fallacy: In this fallacy, a false premise leads to a series of false conclusions. For example: &ldquo;If we don&rsquo;t change the education system, our economy will collapse; if the economy collapses, our society will collapse; therefore, we must change the education system.&rdquo; There is no reasonable logical connection between the premise and the conclusion here.
Metaphysical Fallacy: In this fallacy, an incorrect conclusion is drawn from the meaning of language or words. For example: &ldquo;Money is a form of energy, so if I have enough money, I can control everything.&rdquo;
Appeal to Ignorance: In this fallacy, it is assumed that because something cannot be proven true or false, it must be either true or false. For example: &ldquo;No one can prove the existence of aliens, so aliens must exist.&rdquo;
False Dilemma (or Either/Or Fallacy): In this fallacy, the issue is simplified to only two choices, ignoring other possible options. For example: &ldquo;You either support Party A or support Party B; there is no other choice.&rdquo;
False Continuum (or Continuum Fallacy): In this fallacy, it is incorrectly assumed that there is an infinite continuity between two extremes, ignoring possible intermediate ground. For example: &ldquo;If you don&rsquo;t agree with my view 100%, then you are against my view.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Composition and Fallacy of Division: The fallacy of composition involves applying the characteristics of an individual to an entire group, or applying the characteristics of a group to each individual. For example: &ldquo;The football team of this school is strong, so every student in this school is an excellent athlete.&rdquo;
Gambler&rsquo;s Fallacy: In this fallacy, it is incorrectly assumed that there is a necessary balance between random events, believing that previous results will influence future results. For example: &ldquo;Five heads have appeared in a row when tossing a coin, so the next one must be tails.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Past Belief: In this fallacy, it is assumed that past beliefs or views must be correct. For example: &ldquo;Our ancestors believed the Earth was flat, so the Earth must be flat.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Assuming the Conclusion: In this fallacy, the conclusion is used as a premise to support one&rsquo;s own argument. For example: &ldquo;This book is the Bible of truth because everything it says is true.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Self-Contradiction (or Contradictory Premises): In this fallacy, the premises of the argument contradict each other, leading to an invalid conclusion. For example: &ldquo;I will never lie; right now, I am lying.&rdquo;
Fallacy of Loaded Question: In this fallacy, the question itself is misleading, making it difficult for the respondent to answer without falling into a trap. For example: &ldquo;When will you stop hitting your wife?&rdquo;

The outcome of merely &ldquo;studying&rdquo; without &ldquo;applying&rdquo; (which is the reality for most Chinese students) is that even after more than a decade, they are still studying and still useless (this is the reality for most Chinese students).
The reason we &ldquo;study for a lifetime&rdquo; yet insist on &ldquo;never applying it for a lifetime&rdquo; is because we are afraid of making mistakes. When we make mistakes in childhood, they often lead to punishment. In adulthood, even if there is no punishment from others, there is still the self-deprecation and embarrassment caused by making a mistake. Therefore, many people avoid errors &ldquo;at all costs.&rdquo; However, we must know that the process of acquiring knowledge cannot be separated from trial and error. Without trial and error, comprehensive and genuine progress is impossible. So, you must know that making mistakes is normal, and it is even indispensable. When doing something, making mistakes is inevitable. If you are doing something and there isn&rsquo;t a single mistake, then you aren&rsquo;t doing something—you are dreaming.
“animals are born equal, but some animals are more equal than others.”
There are many benefits to being able to read English fluently. The word &ldquo;freedom&rdquo; basically summarizes the greatest benefit. For example, we can read primary source materials directly, without having to wait a long time to read translations that are often riddled with errors. Translations are often unreliable because translation is truly, truly a difficult job. Often, when a translation is unreliable, it&rsquo;s not the fault of the translator; at least, a portion of the language is fundamentally untranslatable. If you can read English fluently, you will inevitably laugh when reading a translation—many sentences are translated incorrectly, and you can even reconstruct the original English in your mind based on experience. Not relying on translation is a tremendous liberation. When your English reading reaches a certain level of proficiency, you will naturally feel grateful; it is a gratitude worth a thousand pieces of gold. However, we haven&rsquo;t reached that point yet. As for the present, whether you believe it or not is up to you.
Not Knowing Yourself —Because Chinese nouns do not distinguish between singular and plural, and Chinese verbs do not have tenses. After eliminating these two errors, it is equivalent to solving most of the problems.
&ldquo;Consistency Relationship&rdquo;: Subject-verb agreement, tense consistency between the main clause and the subordinate clause, active/passive relationship consistency between non-finite verbs and the logical subject, pronoun reference consistency, consistency of parallel component structure, etc.
As an English user, don&rsquo;t be misled by English teachers. As a teacher, as part of an educational product, they naturally emphasize &ldquo;correct,&rdquo; &ldquo;accurate,&rdquo; and &ldquo;standard&rdquo;—regardless of whether they themselves are truly &ldquo;correct,&rdquo; &ldquo;accurate,&rdquo; or &ldquo;standard.&rdquo; Sometimes it is quite sad to think about; most teachers have to raise their standards not out of a desire for progress, but merely to meet the &ldquo;consumer&rsquo;s&rdquo; often unreasonable expectations. English teachers are human, and thus most of them have the same psychological limitations or human limitations as most other people. When a person cannot do something, they pretend, and keep pretending until they truly believe it themselves.
Another important concern about not daring to speak English is being ridiculed by those around you. This is indeed annoying. But think about it: would a normal person ridicule a baby&rsquo;s stumbling steps? No. Would a normal person ridicule a disabled person? No, and they shouldn&rsquo;t. So look at it: if someone speaks a foreign language with non-standard or unpleasant pronunciation, is it reasonable for them to be ridiculed? If someone speaks a foreign language and carries an accent their whole life, unable to change it, is it reasonable for them to be ridiculed?
&ldquo;Whether they understood&rdquo; is not important here. The goal of significantly increasing auditory input is to rebuild speech recognition ability; the focus is not on textual comprehension ability. Babies have&hellip;
&hellip;already solidified the &ldquo;Bilingual Speech Filter.&rdquo; In fact, I highly suspect that various English early education programs consume too much time that could have been used to acquire other skills, thereby limiting the development of the young child&rsquo;s brain. However, you cannot expect that just by listening to recordings or watching TV, a child will learn a foreign language.
—As long as you start, you will find that it is not difficult, because &ldquo;even if you don&rsquo;t understand, it doesn&rsquo;t matter.&rdquo; The more you listen, the longer you listen, sooner or later, it won&rsquo;t be too hard to understand. Of course, even at the beginning, for better results, you can consciously gradually increase the text difficulty, and it is best to combine it with intensive reading. During this period, almost everyone will feel like there is no progress, but this&hellip;
Mulling Over (or Deep Thinking) The most serious yet most hidden reason for most people failing to learn English might be this: most people have too high an expectation for short-term gains and too low an expectation for long-term gains.
In fact, most people find it difficult to read any sentence fluently in their native language—even though most people believe they can do it. For example, perhaps most people can read any sentence from Reader magazine relatively fluently without any preparation.
But these &ldquo;quick reading&rdquo; suggestions are basically treating the symptoms, not the root cause, because they only solve the problem of input speed, not the problem of comprehension speed after input. No matter how fast the input speed is, if the comprehension speed cannot keep up, what good is it? Sometimes you wonder why so many people clearly think in their minds that they want to improve&hellip;
Picking up a dictionary and looking up words independently is one of the most direct and effective ways to improve reading comprehension.
Those who don&rsquo;t look up words—our bodies&rsquo; every organ possesses a certain &ldquo;evolutionary ability&rdquo; to some degree. If you play the piano for a long time, your fingers become more flexible; if you exercise consistently for a long time, your cardiorespiratory function becomes stronger; if you speak more than others, your tongue becomes more flexible than others.
The biggest difference between English teachers and students might be just one: English teachers must look up words in a dictionary (or other reference books, if there are others), while students have another option besides looking up dictionaries—participating in various courses. What do teachers do in English class? Actually, they mostly just tell the students the words and phrases they looked up the previous night, and the sentences they figured out after thinking about them using the dictionary (and other reference books). And the students? They listen. The students listen, but essentially, they do not participate in the process of reading comprehension.
If you meet a person who doesn&rsquo;t understand that &ldquo;love&rdquo; and &ldquo;marriage&rdquo; are not the same, it is best to keep your distance, otherwise, a painful life &ldquo;is waiting for you not far away&rdquo;&hellip;
If you meet someone who doesn&rsquo;t understand the difference between &ldquo;government&rdquo; and &ldquo;country,&rdquo; it is best not to communicate with them easily, otherwise, you might discover endless troubles and calamities because of it&hellip;
If you don&rsquo;t know that &ldquo;goal&rdquo; and &ldquo;plan&rdquo; are not the same, and you don&rsquo;t know that you might ultimately fail to achieve your goal because of &ldquo;sticking to the plan&rdquo;&hellip;
If you don&rsquo;t know that &ldquo;going to school&rdquo; (上学) and &ldquo;studying&rdquo; (学习) are not the same, some of them might look down on people who graduated from vocational schools because they have a PhD; conversely, others might hate those with PhDs because they only have a vocational diploma&hellip;
If you don&rsquo;t know the difference between &ldquo;a person&rdquo; and &ldquo;a person&rsquo;s view,&rdquo; you might either blindly trust authority or consider yourself to be the absolute authority&hellip;
If you don&rsquo;t know the difference between &ldquo;Action&rdquo; and &ldquo;Reaction,&rdquo; you might spend your whole life as a slave to your surroundings while remaining completely unconscious&hellip;
Many students hate history class; it is actually just that they haven&rsquo;t understood the important difference between &ldquo;history&rdquo; and &ldquo;a history book&rdquo;&hellip;
Organizing vocabulary. You must know that organizing vocabulary yourself after reading an article is far more efficient than memorizing word lists—unfortunately, most people don&rsquo;t believe it.
