Fallacies (or Logical Errors)
1. Appeal to Ignorance (or Argument from Ignorance) When we conclude that something is true simply because it &ldquo;has not been proven false,&rdquo; or conclude that it is false simply because it &ldquo;has not been proven true,&rdquo; our judgment contains a logical flaw. For example, although no one has found evidence of Atlantis&rsquo;s existence yet, this does not mean that Atlantis does not exist.
2. Hasty Generalization When we rush to draw a conclusion based on a small sample, we are prone to committing the fallacy of hasty generalization. &ldquo;I have been to Phoenix three times, and it rained every time, so Phoenix must definitely be a rainy city.&rdquo; (In fact, Phoenix is a desert-like, dry city.)
&ldquo;Because none of the interns in the company have complained that the salary is too low, we can confidently say that the interns in the company are all satisfied with their salaries.&rdquo;
3. Personal Bias When we judge something solely based on personal emotions without considering the actual situation, we are committing the fallacy of personal bias. &ldquo;How can you consult Sheila about marriage problems? Don&rsquo;t you know she was once jailed for email fraud?&rdquo;
4. Poisoning the Well When we judge someone&rsquo;s words too much based on their background, especially their nationality, race, or gender, our logic goes awry. &ldquo;How can your words be taken seriously? You are from Sydney, so of course you think Sydney is better than Melbourne.&rdquo;
5. You&rsquo;re No Better Off If we reject someone&rsquo;s viewpoint simply because they made the same mistake we did, our logic also goes awry. Father: &ldquo;Son, you shouldn&rsquo;t drink. Alcohol damages the liver; how can you be drunk all the time?&rdquo; Son: &ldquo;Dad, aren&rsquo;t you holding a glass of wine right now?&rdquo; Although the son&rsquo;s father is also drinking, this does not mean that his advice to his son is wrong.
6. Red Herring When we try to divert the topic to avoid our own weaknesses during a conversation, we are committing the &ldquo;Red Herring&rdquo; fallacy. (Boss to subordinate): &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t tell me the salary is too low. When I was your age, I only earned $100 a week.&rdquo; The fact that the boss earned a low income when young is irrelevant to the current conversation, because the boss&rsquo;s low income in his youth does not mean the subordinate shouldn&rsquo;t get a raise.
7. Forcing an Irrelevant Conclusion or Function When we reject a rule or plan because it fails to satisfy some unrelated goal, we are committing this logical error. Pete: &ldquo;Do you really think studying logic can solve the problems of this world?&rdquo; Tiffany: &ldquo;Probably not.&rdquo; Pete: &ldquo;Then why are we wasting time studying it?&rdquo;
8. Wishful Thinking When we assume something is true (or false) simply because we desperately wish it to be so, we are committing the &ldquo;Wishful Thinking&rdquo; fallacy. &ldquo;No matter how our team performed before, we will defeat the reigning champion in the first round. Our players are very confident and are determined to achieve a great victory.&rdquo;
9. Appeal to Tradition/Seniority When we try to convince an opponent based on factors like seniority or tradition, rather than facts or evidence, we are committing this logical error. &ldquo;Tim, don&rsquo;t take the idea of going to Annapolis too seriously! Your family—including your father, brothers, grandfather, uncles, etc.—has always been in the military, and they will continue to be in the service. So, young man, your future is at West Point!&rdquo;
10. Appeal to Popular Opinion When we accept or support a viewpoint simply because everyone agrees with it, we are committing this logical error. &ldquo;I am going to vote in favor of the tax amendment. According to a recent public opinion poll, more than two-thirds of registered voters under the age of 25 support the amendment.&rdquo;
11. Pity (or Appeal to Pity) &ldquo;Pity&rdquo; refers to the method of persuading someone by appealing to their sympathy, rather than by presenting factual evidence. (Sheila to John): &ldquo;You must donate to the orphanage. These children are born without knowing who their biological parents are, let alone having food and shelter.&rdquo;

· Either/Or (Dichotomy) Just because there are only two choices presented, one cannot assume that one of them is necessarily correct. Many things are not strictly &ldquo;either/or&rdquo; or &ldquo;black/white.&rdquo; For example: &ldquo;Since you don&rsquo;t support free trade, you must support protectionism!&rdquo;
· Middle Value (Moderation) Many people prefer to take the middle ground between two choices because it avoids either extreme. This practice is sometimes referred to as the Doctrine of the Mean (or Golden Mean). &ldquo;The junior high teacher believes that the school should arrange a fixed curriculum schedule. Meanwhile, the parents believe that students should be able to choose their courses freely. Therefore, the best solution is to combine the two.&rdquo;
· Hodgepodge (Amalgamation) Just because you combine locally correct elements does not necessarily result in a correct overall outcome. &ldquo;Brad is a nice young man, and Janet is an excellent lady; they must be a perfect pair.&rdquo; Marriage is not as simple as &ldquo;1+1=2.&rdquo; Two excellent individuals may not necessarily become a pair of excellent spouses, meaning local correctness does not automatically imply overall correctness.
· Local Error (Partial Error) Just because the whole is correct does not mean that every single part of it is correct. For example, a team may be great, but that does not mean that every player on that team is equally great. &ldquo;Because the car is heavy, all the parts on the car must be heavy.&rdquo; Clearly, this statement is not necessarily true!
· Continuity Gap (Continuity Flaw) Sometimes people feel that certain differences are too minuscule to be worth noting, and this is where the continuity gap appears. &ldquo;Learn one new word every day to improve your vocabulary. Take a medium-sized dictionary and start from the beginning. By learning one new word every day, you will slowly reach the last page. And more importantly, you will learn almost all the important words in English. But how many people can achieve this?&rdquo;
· Attacking the Point (Attacking the Specifics) Many people believe that an inductive argument is easily refuted simply by finding one exception. For instance, a cinema might claim that children under 18 are not allowed entry, but this does not mean that parents cannot bring in a 7-month-old baby to watch the movie. Student A: &ldquo;It is well known that smoking shortens human lifespan.&rdquo; Student B: &ldquo;Yes, but my great-grandfather smoked a pack a day and is still over 90 years old and doing fine. How should we explain that?&rdquo; Finding an exception does not mean the induction is flawed—everything has exceptions, but exceptions are always exceptions, merely low-probability events.
· Distortion (Twisting) Distortion refers to deliberately twisting an opponent&rsquo;s viewpoint in order to achieve the goal of refuting them. Proponent: &ldquo;The only way to improve the education level of developing countries is to provide more material support, such as textbooks.&rdquo; Opponent: &ldquo;Are you suggesting that no matter how many trees you cut down, you still have to print more textbooks?&rdquo;
· Faulty Analogy (False Analogy) We cannot assume that two things are similar (or dissimilar) in other aspects just because they are similar (or dissimilar) in one or a few aspects. &ldquo;Speaking of artificial lures, my favorite is the Rapala lure. This summer, every time I used it, I caught many small bass, so I will definitely use it when fishing for trout in the fall.&rdquo;
· Causal Error (Fallacy of Causation) When we arbitrarily attribute an event to a specific cause, we are committing a causal error. &ldquo;I heard that rich people work very hard, so I must work hard to become rich.&rdquo; Indeed, working hard might make a person wealthy (at least financially), but it is only one factor. We cannot arbitrarily assume that hard work alone is sufficient to turn a pauper into a rich person.
· Domino Error (Domino Effect Fallacy) We cannot assume that if one event triggers another, it will necessarily trigger a subsequent series of events. The domino error is sometimes also called the &ldquo;Chain Reaction Error.&rdquo; &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t oppose providing free food to the homeless, but since we provide free food, we will need to provide free clothes, and then free accommodation. Soon, we will have to provide them with a fixed annual salary.&rdquo;
· Gambler&rsquo;s Fallacy When we judge the probability of a future event based on past occurrences—and these two events are completely independent and unrelated—we are committing the Gambler&rsquo;s Fallacy. (Parents to doctor): &ldquo;Since we already have three boys, I believe the next one must be a girl.&rdquo; The result of the last birth has absolutely no bearing on the result of the next birth; it is certain that the probability of the next birth being a boy or a girl is still 50/50.
· Faulty Precision (Misleading Precision) Faulty precision refers to arbitrarily citing numbers that appear precise but are actually baseless in fact to prove one&rsquo;s point. &ldquo;In Shakespeare&rsquo;s time, one in four people disliked Shakespeare&rsquo;s plays.&rdquo; The speaker could not possibly obtain such precise data, especially data from the Shakespearean era over four hundred years ago.

